<p>What follows is reproduced in John's own words, exactly as he wrote them: nothing edited, softened, or left out. It carries the same broad, joking, tall-tale narrator he uses throughout "Please Go to Hell" and "Once Around the Thunder Mug," a folksy small-town-goofball persona that was a deliberate comic choice, not a reflection of how he actually wrote or spoke. The scans were read twice by machine and the doubtful words checked against the page images. Where a slip appears below, it is a slip in the printed book itself (for example "The didn't know" for "They didn't know" in the Foreword, and "it's" for "its" in the poem on the first page), and it has been kept as printed. Numbers in parentheses are the book's own printed page numbers.</p>

<p>John signed his newspaper column and this and several later self-published books with the pen name "Thundermug." The cover and title page print the title as two words, "Thunder Mug"; the book's running text and its table of contents print it as one, "Thundermug." Both are kept as printed.</p>

