<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>

  <p>In our first literary effort, <em>Once Around the Thundermug</em>, we made a brief trip through the "growing up" years of a group of kids lucky enough to be born in a town called Creede and in the best age any generation of humans could hope to live. It was an era where water was drawn from wells with a dip bucket and a biffy stood proudly a short distance from each house. We were acquainted with the old timers who created the boom with hard work and mining savvy and we learned lessons from the roughest of the miners and teamsters as well as the gentlest of the ladies. Some of the lessons were not appreciated by our parents.</p>

  <p>There were inaccuracies in the first publication and we apologize for them. I tried to make a Hollander of Elsie Broadhead and learned she came directly from Scotland. Joanne Tankersley has done a painstaking job of researching history of Mineral County oldsters and brought this to my attention. My thanks to her.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The correction refers to the Mrs. Broadhead of "Once Around the Thunder Mug," Chapter II, whom John describes there as "this squat, gentle lady with the low voice and slight Dutch accent" (see <a href="/writings/once-around-the-thunder-mug/of-bagpipes-coasting-and-firemen/#grandparents-the-building-blocks">"Grandparents, the Building Blocks"</a>). That page has not been changed; the two books are kept exactly as printed, and this note ties them together. The book does not say who Joanne Tankersley is. "Echoes from the Thundermug" mentions a Joanne, also called Joan, who was the wife of Seth E. "Tank" Tankersley, but nothing here confirms that she is the same woman.</em></p>

  <p>I have never been overly accurate with words or firearms. I tried to kill an older brother in the aftermath of a serious argument in the early '30's. I lost the argument and I missed the first shot standing and the next two running as he was in low silhouette. The light was poor in the room and the rear sight was elevated.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>John does not name the brother. As printed, "the early '30's" reads as a decade, but John was born in 1927, which would make him a small child in the early 1930s, so he may mean his own early thirties, the late 1950s. The book gives no other date, and the passage is kept exactly as printed.</em></p>

  <p>I will admit, also, that I have never been overly truthful. I tried very hard, the first year of our marriage, to be open and truthful with my bride, but I soon learned that women will believe a poorly conceived lie before they will accept a truth sworn to on a stack of Bibles. It is just their nature and it has worked to my advantage to hold the truth in reserve in case all else fails.</p>

  <p>With apologies and admissions in order we shall protect ourselves further by declaring the following stories to be truths, half truths and outright lies. We will be held accountable only for the half truths.</p>

  <p>I spent the first 50 years of my life making every error humanly possible, some of them three or four times. I spent the next 10 years hoping I was the only one who knew and the years since trying to rectify the worst of the errors.</p>

  <p>Our priorities change too fast as we age. At 6 years of age I knew the most important thing in the world was to own a .22 caliber Winchester rifle. At 10 years of age a bicycle put the rifle on a back burner.</p>

  <p>I wasted all those years setting useless priorities when the real necessities boil down to indoor plumbing and soft toilet paper. Both were a long time coming in the little mining town of Creede, Colorado.</p>

  <p>While working with a crew of oldsters at the Commodore Mine I was full of piss and vinegar and showing off at every opportunity. I slapped a 300 pound length of rail into place and was starting to fishplate it to another when John Slater looked at me and said, "You're a real hot-shot, Kid, but you'll see the day when a good bowel movement will be the most exciting thing you can look forward to." He was right.</p>

  <p>If you visited Creede in the '30's and came in from the east, the first place you would pass on the left belonged to a venerable prospector named Jack Fleming. Jack was a born optimist and a favorite of mine. When gold prices improved he had a foot of gold ore on the footwall. When silver prices were high he had a foot of silver ore on the hanging wall and when uranium became a target he told me he had a foot of you-ran-ee-um between the gold and silver ores. He had lost an eye and a foot in the trade he loved with a passion, but he was always the first to head for the hills and the last to return.</p>

  <p>If you visited Creede from the west, the first buildings you passed belonged to the Allens. They were grouped under the embankment where the road inclines to the lower end of town. On making the turn to Creede proper, the first cabin on the left belonged to Sam Funk who was watchman for the Sulphur Mill, a large concrete structure, now utilized by Mineral County. Mr. Funk could speak the Ute tongue very well having journeyed to the area when the Ute tribe still had encampments in the valley. The sulphur mine had closed, but there was much raw ore still in the mill which was put to good use in fumigating homes infested with bedbugs, a scourge at that time.</p>

  <p>An early morning ritual of upper Creede businessmen was rolling out the awnings. They were all very colorful and probably impressed me more than any other sight. One could hear laughter and good natured kidding from sidewalk to sidewalk as Mr. Joel Eaves lowered his candy striped canvas followed by John "Hoot" Owsley and across the street Sis Parsons would be fitting his crank to the slot. Up Main Street, near the firehouse, Stanley Parker would shade the windows of his drug-store as Wallace Leary lowered the awning of Tomkins Hardware Company, a large building also housing the bank.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>John describes the same morning ritual, and the same two men, Mr. Eaves and Mr. Owsley, in "Echoes from the Thundermug," <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/creede-boyhood-part-one/#the-magic-age-is-five">"The Magic Age Is Five,"</a> with different details: there Mr. Eaves cranks out a "red and white striped awning" just before Mr. Owsley lowers an "orange awning."</em></p>

  <p>Hoot Owsley was a favorite with locals and tourists alike. He and his wife, Kate, were fishing for trout every free minute and Hoot thrilled the tourists with stories of the big ones, not the ones that got away, but the ones he caught and he had proof in his pictures. They had a large Studebaker sedan to transport them to their favorite fishing holes. They were an impressive pair when decked out in fishing gear and Kate, with her ever present bonnet tied with a wide red ribbon under her chin, smiling and waving to all they passed.</p>

  <p>The town was fairly self-sufficient during those years and we would probably have survived had we been isolated by both road and rail closures. We were very appreciative of the fruit trucks that came in late summer and meats from packing houses afforded a change from our main staples of trout and venison. Dr. McKibbin and practical nurses could handle nearly any emergency and patients had a high threshold of pain. I remember Dr. McKibbin sending for my Father when a sheep-herder rode in with a hole in his belly. He had picked up a blasting cap at the Resurrection Mine on his way back to his herd. The details were never clear, but it had gone off in his watch pocket tearing flesh and embedding bits of cloth and belt in the wound. Dad and the doctor's son held the poor fellow as the entrails were cleaned and the torn flesh stitched. He made a full recovery and visited again the next year. Our next practitioner, Dr. Sprecher, disliked adenoids and tonsils. His office was over the Economy Grocery where the glands of many Creede children were removed and dumped in Willow Creek, the nearest receptacle. Each patient received a bowl of ice cream when able to sit up after surgery. For the most part the treat was promptly thrown up, along with swallowed blood, and also consigned to Willow Creek.</p>
</section>

